        <!-- Firestore Rules Section -->
        <div id="firestore-rules" class="section hidden">
          <h2>Firestore Security Rules</h2>
          <p>Your app enforces strict security rules that ensure users can only access their own data.</p>

          <h3>Current Rules</h3>
          <div class="code-block">
rules_version = '2';

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /tasks/{taskId} {
      // Read: User must be authenticated AND own the task
      allow read: if request.auth != null
        && resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid;

      // Create: User must be authenticated AND set their own uid
      allow create: if request.auth != null
        && request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid;

      // Update: User must own the task
      allow update: if request.auth != null
        && resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid
        && request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid;

      // Delete: User must own the task
      allow delete: if request.auth != null
        && resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid;
    }
  }
}
          </div>

          <h3>Rule Explanation</h3>
          <p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Every operation checks <code>resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid</code></p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Read:</strong> Fetch tasks where <code>uid</code> matches your user ID</li>
            <li><strong>Create:</strong> New tasks must have your user ID as <code>uid</code></li>
            <li><strong>Update:</strong> Can only modify tasks you created</li>
            <li><strong>Delete:</strong> Can only remove tasks you created</li>
          </ul>

          <div class="info-box">
            <strong>No cross-user access:</strong> Even if you know someone else's UID, you cannot read, modify, or delete their tasks. The database enforces this automatically.
          </div>

